    "notes": "Complete connected Palais Bourbon parliamentary exterior: twelve-column river portico, tall solid blank/original pediment and broad stairs, rounded chamber roof, four-column ceremonial-court portico, both returned court wings, complete connected administrative/library ranges around all three actual courtyard holes, corner pavilions, mansard/dormer bands, open balustrades and closed recessed glazing/doors. Native north porch, court porch and south colonnade remain open between real columns. Separate Hôtel de Lassay frontage53, gallery52, ministries, city, gardens and artwork remain independent. No copied statuary, heraldry, lettering or interiors. Approximate standard global DEM reference36m. Common palace floor38.6m; north portico platform40.6m and broad steps reach the unchanged native forecourt. Current native3D parts guide heights:27.4m pediment,22.7m rounded chamber roof,18.4m court hall and14.8m administrative roofs relative to datum36m. These are map/picture-guided approximate heights, not surveyed historic thresholds. Rear grades vary; actual native contact is checked in current renders. Broad stairs follow17 actual native-field samples across their width; skyline/district use5 and street/close-up9 of those samples. The deep court porch is carved out of the parent shell and has an original supported platform/closed backdrop. +X WNW along the twelve-column Seine facade; +Z NNE toward the river/front portico; -Z SSW toward ceremonial and administrative courts. +Y up."
